Norfolk mental health advocate Charlotte  Underwood who self-harmed from the ages  of 14 to 20 Charlotte very good morning  to you and thanks coming out to talk  about this I think you're 22 now and why  did you begin self-harm and what form  did it take I didn't start self harming  on purpose it was something that just  develops after feeling quite anxious and  alone I was quite heavy in my cutting  but it was because I had no healthy  outlet and I just didn't know where else  to turn what do you say to those people  who heard what you just said and say  well anxiety and loneliness have been  part and parcel of being a teenager for  as long as there have been teenagers but  people didn't used to resort to to  cutting as you say I think it's more to  do with the way the world has changed  and the lack of communication between  all of us really and at the same time  there is more to self harm than just  cutting so whether it didn't exist  before it's definitely questionable when  you when you say we don't talk to each  other anymore I think I paraphrase what  you said there is that is that a  function of social media that we lots of  young people now exist in her in a  bubble that's created by their online  life definitely something I always say  is that in a world of mass communication  we've learned how to properly  communicate and I think that applies  quite well to that was there anything  specifically which which prompted you to  do it was the one event was it an  accumulation of things it was sorry it  was an accumulation of feeling very  unwell for a long time but not  understanding what was going on in  myself so I had a mental illness but I  didn't know I had it and that was  terrifying  what did it take for you to break the  cycle did it require outside help or is  it just something you were able to work  through yourself it's something that  took eight years of perseverance and  accepting the ups and downs and learning  to talk about it  and get those feelings out of my head  and finding healthy outlets rather than  the not-so-good ones like so far yeah  really this morning a bit more about  this  words like romanticizing glamourizing  self-harm which seemed completely  paradoxical how could it how is it even  possible to glamorize self harming is it  I don't think it is I think it's again  it's that stigma where people are maybe  trying to avoid talking about it and  spin it into something even worse than  it is if you are somebody perhaps on the  brink of going back to school maybe  thinking hard about exams that are  looming and you think that perhaps this  is a an avenue you may consider going  down if you're a young person a teenager  a particular girl because this is  something which we're told affects girls  far more than boys what's your message  to somebody who's who's contemplating  doing something like this you never know  what someone's gonna say to you if you  tell them that you need help you don't  have to suffer alone people will listen  and you can learn to not self harm or  prevent it and I guess stop those  thoughts just by having a healthy outlet  and having someone to talk to Charlotte  you've talked to us this morning we  appreciate it thanks very much indeed
 

